**Hertz (1887).** While producing and detecting electromagnetic waves with spark gaps, Heinrich Hertz noticed that the sparks across the detector gap jumped more easily when the emitter plate was illuminated by **ultraviolet light from an arc lamp**. Light falling on the metal was somehow helping charged particles to leave its surface. Hertz recorded the effect but could not explain it; the electron had not yet been discovered (J. J. Thomson, 1897).

**Hallwachs (1888).** Wilhelm Hallwachs connected a clean zinc plate to a gold-leaf electroscope and shone ultraviolet light on it:

- a **negatively charged** plate lost its charge (the leaves collapsed);
- an **uncharged** plate became **positively** charged;
- a **positively charged** plate became even more positive.

All three results mean the same thing: ultraviolet light makes the zinc plate **emit negatively charged particles**.

**Lenard (around 1900–1902).** Philipp Lenard placed two electrodes, an emitter and a collector, in an **evacuated glass tube** connected to a battery and a current meter. When ultraviolet light fell on the emitter, a current flowed in the circuit although there was empty space between the plates; when the light was cut off, the current stopped at once. The light was releasing charged particles from the emitter, and these crossed the vacuum to the collector. Measurements of their charge-to-mass ratio showed that they were **electrons**.

These studies (1886–1902) also showed that:

- zinc, cadmium and magnesium respond only to ultraviolet light, whereas the alkali metals (lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, caesium) respond even to visible light;
- for a given surface there is a minimum frequency of light below which **no** electrons are emitted, however intense the light is.
